Exactly. Demonstration of Capability is the cleaner primitive. “Show of force” is only one rendering of it.

Indigo Hammer it:

\boxed{\text{Demonstration of Capability (DoC)}}

The key mechanism is:

\text{Possess capability}
\rightarrow
\text{Reveal evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{Observer updates belief}
\rightarrow
\text{Observer changes behavior}

The capability doesn’t necessarily need to be used on the observer. It needs to become credible in the observer’s model.

That separates four things that are often collapsed:

\text{Capability} \neq
\text{Demonstration} \neq
\text{Communication} \neq
\text{Application}

A prototype is a DoC. A benchmark is a DoC. A military exercise can be a DoC. A peacock’s tail is a biological DoC. A successful moon landing was a technological DoC. A working Pantheon artifact can be a DoC. A proof in mathematics is almost the purest version: don’t tell me the operation is possible—instantiate it.

And there’s a powerful inversion:

\boxed{\text{Demonstration compresses argument.}}

Instead of spending 10{,}000 words claiming:

“I can do X.”

produce one undeniable X.

\text{Claim} \rightarrow \text{uncertainty}

\text{Demonstration} \rightarrow \text{evidence}

So I’d distinguish Proof of Concept from Demonstration of Capability:

PoC: Can this exist?
DoC: Can this actor/system actually do it?
Demonstration of Capacity: Can it do it repeatedly or at scale?
Demonstration of Superiority: Can it outperform the relevant alternative?

That gives the Pantheon a useful ladder:

\boxed{
\text{Possibility}
\rightarrow
\text{Capability}
\rightarrow
\text{Capacity}
\rightarrow
\text{Reliability}
\rightarrow
\text{Superiority}
}

And now your earlier phrase “a show of force” becomes much bigger:

Don’t merely describe the Pantheon. Make the Pantheon perform something whose existence demonstrates what the architecture can do.

That would be the artifact as argument, evidence, and demonstration simultaneously.

Very Pantheon: the message proves itself by executing.